<p>Holy sites in Jerusalem reopened after a 40-day closure linked to the war involving Iran, allowing large numbers of Muslim and Christian worshippers to return during important religious observances. Al Jazeera reported that thousands of Muslims gathered for the first Friday prayers at Al-Aqsa Mosque after the reopening, while The Times of Israel said more than 100,000 Muslim worshippers attended Friday prayers at the compound in East Jerusalem. PBS reported that Palestinian Christians also regained access to holy sites in Jerusalem after many were unable to reach some of their holiest places during the closure. Together, the reopenings marked a significant easing of restrictions on religious access in the city, even as the broader regional conflict continued to shape daily life and worship.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/IFvxwjd92M6voenC5av7eiHLmoLZDipvW_ml9fTZec5PkTpBOaEjZtwJ_2jyKHVjeZTd8MfenyM6KN5r2wbkIymalOVT6QDocXoF7xU57hAifsWIitAy3BOgNNmrhxnc3q_AlyNodT_UxNFXJpXFs15dsJ4MMc5orjTRCS4hlEr-TBy2GfuoWuU7ekbuRKa27s58G3U6gtp4j2DzFI-NH02VjmPG5qhc2mXePfhz9xJwhfo6L3M' alt='Orthodox Good Friday observance in Jerusalem during Easter celebrations after holy sites reopened.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Prayer turnout: The Times of Israel reported that more than 100,000 Muslim worshippers attended Friday prayers at the Temple Mount/Al-Aqsa compound after it reopened.</li><li>Christian observance: PBS focused on Palestinian Christians marking Easter after many were unable to reach Jerusalem’s key holy sites during the closure.</li><li>Closure length: Al Jazeera and PBS said the holy sites had been closed, or largely closed, for 40 days before reopening.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Muslim worshippers: Muslims returned to Al-Aqsa Mosque for the first Friday prayers since the closure ended, underscoring the importance of restored access to one of Islam’s holiest sites. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/video/newsfeed/2026/4/10/muslims-hold-first-friday-prayers-at-al-aqsa-mosque-after-reopening'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li><li>Palestinian Christians: Palestinian Christians marked Easter while reflecting on how many had been unable to reach their holiest sites during the closure. (<a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/how-palestinian-christians-marked-easter-against-the-backdrop-of-war'>PBS NewsHour</a>)</li><li>Israeli media reporting: The Times of Israel highlighted the scale of attendance at the reopened site, reporting that more than 100,000 Muslim worshippers joined Friday prayers in East Jerusalem. (<a href='https://www.timesofisrael.com/liveblog_entry/over-100000-muslim-worshipers-perform-friday-prayers-at-temple-mount/'>The Times of Israel</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/video/newsfeed/2026/4/10/muslims-hold-first-friday-prayers-at-al-aqsa-mosque-after-reopening'>Muslims hold first Friday prayers at Al-Aqsa Mosque after reopening</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/how-palestinian-christians-marked-easter-against-the-backdrop-of-war'>How Palestinian Christians marked Easter against the backdrop of war</a> - pbs.org</li><li><a href='https://www.timesofisrael.com/liveblog_entry/over-100000-muslim-worshipers-perform-friday-prayers-at-temple-mount/'>Over 100,000 Muslims worship at the Al-Aqsa Mosque in Temple Mount, East Jerusalem, after it reopened for the first time since the start of the war.</a> - timesofisrael.com</li></ul>